<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qutb ud-Din Aibak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1150–<a href="/years/1210">1210</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-dynasty-delhi">Mamluk dynasty (Delhi)</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qutb ud-Din Aibak was a Turkic slave-general of the Ghurid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a>, who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> as viceroy from 1192. After his master's death he declared himself independent ruler, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-dynasty-delhi">Slave Dynasty</a>. He is regarded as the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and began the construction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-minar">Qutb Minar</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-aibak.jpg" alt="Qutb ud-Din Aibak" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Muhammad Umair Mirza · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grave_of_Sultan_Qutb_al-Din_Aibak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aibak came from Turkestan and belonged to a Turkic tribe of the same name, a word derived from the Turkic terms for moon and lord. Separated from his family as a child, he was taken to the slave market of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>, where a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazi">qazi</a>, Fakhruddin Abdul Aziz Kufi, bought him. He was treated affectionately, educated with the qazi's sons and taught archery, horse-riding and Qur'an recital. He was resold to a merchant, who sold him to the Ghurid Sultan Muhammad Ghori at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>. He rose to be Amir-i Akhur, officer of the royal stables, and after being captured by Khwarazmian scouts and held in an iron cage he was released and favoured by the Sultan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Aibak served at the first and second battles of Tarain, and after the Ghurid victory of 1192 Muhammad Ghori placed him over the former Chahamana territory at Kuhram. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meerut">Meerut</a>, Baran and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1192; in 1193 he deposed the Tomara ruler there and took direct control. He fought in the campaign against the Gahadavala king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayachandra">Jayachandra</a>, defeated the Chaulukya army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-abu">Mount Abu</a> in 1197 and plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-gujarat">Anhilwara</a>, and besieged Kalinjar in 1202. His campaigns involved the large-scale capture of slaves; Hasan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami</a> gives 20,000 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and 50,000 for Kalinjar, figures that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irfan-habib">Irfan Habib</a> thinks exaggerated.</p><p>After Muhammad Ghori was assassinated in March 1206, the citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> asked Aibak to take sovereign power, and he informally ascended the throne on 25 June 1206. He disputed Ghurid territory with his father-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-al-din-yildiz">Taj al-Din Yildiz</a> and at one point took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>. In 1208 to 1209 Ghiyasuddin Mahmud sent him a ceremonial parasol and a deed recognising him as ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1210 Aibak fell from his horse while playing chaugan, a form of polo, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and died when the pommel of the saddle pierced his ribs. He left no named heir. Turkic officers appointed Aram Shah, who ruled no more than eight months before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltutmish">Iltutmish</a>, Aibak's former slave and son-in-law, defeated him and made the loosely held territories into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. The line is called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> or Slave dynasty, though only Aibak, Iltutmish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balban">Balban</a> were slaves.</p><p>Contemporary chroniclers praised his generosity, which earned him the epithet lakh-bakhsh. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-minar">Qutb Minar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> was begun in his reign, and the Adhai Din Ka Jhonpra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> is also attributed to him. His tomb is in Anarkali Bazaar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qutb ud-Din Aibak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Qutb ud-Din Aibak</b>, a Turkic slave-general who had governed the Ghurid conquests in northern India from Delhi since the victory at Tarain in 1192, assumed independent power at Lahore and founded the Mamluk or Slave Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The first sultan of Delhi, <b>Qutb ud-Din Aibak</b>, was a former slave; most Abbasid caliphs were sons of enslaved concubines; and the shahs of Safavid Iran built a force of Caucasian captives, trained as their own slaves, the ghulams, who took over much of the army and many…</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>Some enslaved men rose high in the sultans’ service, as <b>Qutb ud-Din Aibak</b>, a former slave, rose to be the first sultan of Delhi in 1206.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Qutb al-Din Aibak</b> attacked Nadol in 1197, ending the main line.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-naddula">Chahamanas of Naddula</a></li><li><blockquote>Prithviraja III of the Chahamanas of Shakambhari defeated Paramardi in 1182, and in 1203 <b>Qutb al-Din Aibak</b> took Kalinjar.</blockquote><a href="/state/chandela-dynasty">Chandela dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Mularaja II defeated Muhammad of Ghor in 1178, but <b>Qutb al-Din Aibak</b> sacked Anahilavada in 1197.</blockquote><a href="/state/chaulukya-dynasty">Chaulukya dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.3,63.9&amp;year=1189" aria-label="Central Asia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Qutb ud-Din Aibak"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5234 6237h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5234 6237h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.3,63.9&amp;year=1189">What was here in 1189? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
